{
  "generated_text": "To solve the equation \\((2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\\), we will follow a step-by-step approach.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equation for clarity:\n\\[\n(2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(4^x\\) can be rewritten as \\((2^2)^x = (2^x)^2\\). Let's set \\(y = 2^x\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\n(y - 2)(5y + 10) = 3y^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nNext, expand the left-hand side:\n\\[\n(y - 2)(5y + 10) = y(5y + 10) - 2(5y + 10)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5y^2 + 10y - 10y - 20\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5y^2 - 20\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute this back into the equation:\n\\[\n5y^2 - 20 = 3y^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(y\\), first isolate the terms involving \\(y\\) on one side:\n\\[\n5y^2 - 3y^2 - 20 = 12\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^2 - 20 = 12\n\\]\n\nNext, add 20 to both sides:\n\\[\n2y^2 = 32\n\\]\n\nThen, divide both sides by 2:\n\\[\ny^2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\ny = 4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = -4\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = 2^x\\). Since \\(2^x\\) must be positive for all real \\(x\\), we discard \\(y = -4\\). Thus:\n\\[\n2^x = 4\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(4\\) can be written as a power of 2:\n\\[\n4 = 2^2\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\n2^x = 2^2\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we equate the exponents:\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
